        "descriptions": [
          {
            "lang": "en-Us",
            "description": "With the rise of the Internet and social media platforms, people now have access to more weather information than ever before. Although this allows end users to receive weather information from a variety of sources, the sheer volume of available information likely contributes to a perception that weather risk messages are inconsistent. Members of the weather enterprise share these concerns and believe that these perceived inconsistencies may negatively affect their many audiences. The challenge, however, is that there are only a few studies to-date that have explored message consistency in a weather context. Therefore, in its current state, the weather enterprise lacks empirical evidence that (1) demonstrates that message consistency is a relevant operational concern and (2) provides research-guided recommendations to practitioners and operational meteorologists on how to achieve a more consistent message. To address this operational need, this research project conceptualized ‘message consistency’ for the weather enterprise and employs a social science mixed-methods approach to explore the effects of weather-related graphical inconsistencies on lay public end users. In particular, this project used the Storm Prediction Center’s (SPC) Convective Outlook graphic as a vehicle to investigate the role that visual design plays in keeping a weather-related message consistent.",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          },
          {
            "lang": "en-Us",
            "description": "The first mission offered rich qualitative feedback on how members of the public evaluate weather-related graphics to determine whether they communicate the same message. More importantly, Study 1 revealed that when operational meteorologists alter specific graphical elements in a Convective Outlook (e.g., risk areas, colors, etc.), they also—albeit unintentionally—likely affect the susceptibility and severity information in the process. This finding points to the need for more research that explores the hypothesized relationships to better understand whether the specific graphical elements participants mentioned affect perceived susceptibility and severity, and more broadly, investigates whether these specific graphical elements impact people’s perceived message consistency. The remaining studies will do just that. However, before exploring those hypothesized relationships, this mission used an experimental survey methodology to manipulate the graphical elements mentioned in the first mission to better understand the effects on perceived message consistency. This also served as a manipulation check before conducting additional studies.\n\nAs a first step toward quantifying the effects of inconsistent or conflicting graphic weather information, five inconsistencies associated with the Convective Outlook graphic were selected using the findings from Study 1 (see above). Each inconsistency corresponded to a graphical element that members of the public emphasized when asked to describe why two Convective Outlook graphics did not communicate the same message. The five graphical elements included: changes in (1) a location’s risk category, (2) color scheme, (3) risk language, (4) geographic scale, and (5) number of risk categories.\n\nA PowerPoint template was used to create hypothetical Convective Outlook graphics that feature each graphical inconsistency (Figure 3). This template was given to the research team by a local broadcast meteorologist; therefore, the graphics that were created for this study were similar to those used by other operational and broadcast meteorologists in the weather, water, and climate enterprise. Not only did this improve ecological validity, but it also allowed the research team to systematically manipulate the Convective Outlook graphics based on the five graphical elements described above. Additionally, each manipulated graphic was paired with an identical reference graphic (Figure 3). This design was purposeful, as it allowed for the direct comparison of perceived consistency across the five experimental conditions.\n\nAfter agreeing to participate in the online experiment for extra credit, 655 undergraduate students received a hypothetical weather scenario explaining that meteorologists were forecasting the threat for severe weather for their location on a Saturday afternoon. After reading the scenario, participants were randomly assigned to one of the five experimental conditions where they were shown a reference graphic (which all participants saw) and a manipulated Convective Outlook graphic based on one of the experimental conditions. This was followed by a measure of perceived consistency and demographic questions. However, because message consistency is a relatively new concept in the weather enterprise, an established scale for measuring the concept did not exist. Therefore, the research team developed a perceived consistency scale based on a review of interdisciplinary social science research and the definition of message consistency offered by Williams and Eosco (2021).",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          }
        ],
